SECTION 2 -- REQUIREMENTS (a), (c), (d) ON THE (b)-SURVIVORS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [FAIL] a1 (a) q_det transforms as a spatial SCALAR under spatial diffeomorphisms, delta_xi q = xi.dq   (it does not.  Computing delta_xi q = -(1/6) gamma^ij (Lie_xi gamma)_ij directly gives delta_xi q - xi.dq + (1/3) d_k xi^k = 0 exactly, i.e. the anomaly delta_xi q_det = xi.dq - (1/3) d_k xi^k that L12 reports, reproduced here independently)
  [PASS] a2 (a) a curvature scalar really is anomaly-free: R3 of the SAME 3-geometry written in two radial charts (r and rho = r - M) gives the same number at the same point, while q_det does not   (R3 agrees exactly under the chart change (True); q_det does not (False).  Every (b)-survivor here (R4, I1, I2, E^2, Killing norm, q_ratio, T) is a spacetime scalar and so satisfies (a) by the same argument -- requirement (a) is not what decides this search)

    (c) THE STATIC WEAK FIELD.  Does the survivor reduce to the Newtonian potential, for a
        GENERAL source (i.e. does it superpose), so that |D qt| is an acceleration?
        reference system: M_b = 6e10 Msun at 10 kpc, g_N = 8.365e-11 m/s^2
          canonical : a0 = 9.3619e-11, y = g_N/a0 = 0.894, deep-MOND g_obs ~ sqrt(g_N a0) = 8.849e-11 m/s^2
          alt       : a0 = 1.1279e-10, y = g_N/a0 = 0.742, deep-MOND g_obs ~ sqrt(g_N a0) = 9.713e-11 m/s^2
  [FAIL] c08 (c) R4 (and every Ricci-built scalar: R_munu R^munu, T, rho) reduces to the Newtonian potential in the static weak field   (it does not: R4^(1) = 2 grad^2 Phi (control C4), so R4 = 0 identically in VACUUM -- outside the baryons, which is exactly where the rotation curve lives.  c^2|D R4| = 0 against a required 8.849e-11 (canonical) / 9.713e-11 (alt) m/s^2 at 10 kpc: the deficit is total, on both footings)
  [FAIL] c09 (c) I1 = Kretschmann, I2 = grad-Riemann squared (equivalently E_ij E^ij, C^2) reduce to the Newtonian potential   (it does not: I1 = 48 M^2/r^6 is TIDAL.  The only dimensionless combination with the right radial fall-off is I1^{1/6} ~ M^(1/3)/r, so c^2|D qt| ~ M^(1/3)/r^2 -- calibrate on a 6e10 Msun spiral and a 6e8 Msun dwarf is predicted 22x too strong; v_c^2 = g r ~ M^(1/3)/r still falls off, so there is no flat rotation curve and no BTFR on either footing.  I2 = 720 M^2(r-2M)/r^9 carries the same disease: both are quadratic in the TIDAL field, not linear in the potential)
  [FAIL] c11 (c) E_ij E^ij, the electric Weyl, reduces to the Newtonian potential   (it does not, for the same reason (E_ij = d_i d_j Phi in the weak field is the TIDAL tensor, two derivatives of the potential, not the potential); and it additionally vanishes wherever the spacetime is conformally flat -- see T5 below)

        c13 THE SUPERPOSITION TEST for q_ratio.  It is exact for ONE point mass by construction
            (control C5).  Requirement (c) is about a GENERAL static source, so: two point masses.
            field point                        c^2 q_ratio           Phi     q/Phi  c^2|Dq_ratio|   |grad Phi|     ratio
            midpoint, 0.5 kpc off-axis         -2.8256e+11   -1.7122e+10    16.502     3.7170e-08   1.0988e-11   3382.76
            on-axis, 2 kpc from centre         -1.9279e+10   -2.0486e+10     0.941     7.0233e-11   1.2645e-10      0.56
            perpendicular, 8 kpc out           -8.1570e+09   -9.1202e+09     0.894     4.5936e-11   2.6567e-11      1.73
            far field, 200 kpc out             -4.3047e+08   -4.3006e+08     1.001     6.9839e-14   6.9643e-14      1.00
  [FAIL] c13 (c) q_ratio superposes, i.e. reduces to the Newtonian potential for a GENERAL static source and not merely for the single Schwarzschild solution it was tuned on   (it does not: for two equal 1e10 Msun masses 10 kpc apart, q_ratio/Phi runs from 0.894 to 16.502 across the field and the predicted ACCELERATION runs from 0.56x to 3383x the Newtonian one.  It is exact only in the far field, where the two-body system looks like the single point mass it was tuned on.  A ratio of curvature invariants is not additive, so it is not a potential)

        c12 THE KILLING-NORM CANDIDATE.  ln sqrt(-xi.xi) = (1/2) ln(1 - 2M/r) -> -M/r = Phi/c^2,
            and because it EQUALS Phi/c^2 at linear order it superposes exactly.
  [PASS] c12 (c) ln sqrt(-xi.xi) reduces to the Newtonian potential in the static weak field, for a general static source   (yes: log((-2*M + r)/r)/2 = -M/r + O(M^2) = Phi/c^2, and Phi obeys a linear Poisson equation so it superposes by construction -- this candidate PASSES (c))

    (d) EMPTY FLAT SPACE, IN EVERY COORDINATE SYSTEM.
  [PASS] d1 (d) the (b)-surviving GEOMETRIC scalars (R4, I1, I2, E^2, Killing norm) vanish in genuinely empty flat space in BOTH Cartesian and spherical coordinates   (yes -- R4 = I1 = 0 exactly in both charts, and ln sqrt(-xi.xi) = ln 1 = 0.  This is the requirement q_det fails catastrophically (control C3b, -1.6e3 kg/m^3 at 1 AU))
  [FAIL] d2 (d) q_ratio is well defined and vanishes in genuinely empty flat space   (it is not: I1 = I2 = 0 in flat space, so sqrt(I1^3)/I2 is 0/0 -- undefined, not zero.  Every curvature-RATIO construction has this disease, because the ratio exists only where curvature does)

SECTION 3 -- THE STRUCTURAL TENSION, ADDRESSED HEAD ON: IS IT A THEOREM?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    The tension the assignment names is real and it has now been made precise.  Three statements, each
    proved on the geometry above rather than asserted.

    T1  THE DERIVATIVE-COUNT THEOREM.  Requirement (c) asks for the ZEROTH-derivative object Phi;
        requirements (a)+(b) force at least the SECOND.  Nothing sits in between.
        vacuum weak field (Phi harmonic): linearised R_munu = 0 -> True;
        the only surviving linear invariant is R^(1)_(0i0j) = d_i d_j Phi -> True
  [FAIL] T1 [theorem] some natural dimensionless scalar has a part LINEAR in h_munu that equals Phi itself, i.e. zero derivatives of the potential   (none does.  A natural scalar's linear part must be invariant under h -> h + d_(mu xi_nu) (control C4), and the complete set of such invariants is the LINEARISED RIEMANN TENSOR, which is exactly two derivatives of h.  Computed here: in vacuum the linearised Ricci tensor vanishes identically (True), so every Ricci-built scalar is zero there, and the one surviving piece is R^(1)_(0i0j) = d_i d_j Phi (True) -- the TIDAL tensor, dimension 1/L^2.  Making it dimensionless needs either an external length (giving l^2 grad^2 Phi, not Phi) or a RATIO of invariants, which is not linear at all -- and the ratio branch is exactly candidate b13, killed on superposition (c13) and on 0/0 in flat space (d2).  Both horns are closed)

    T4  THE SLICE-METRIC NO-GO.  Any qt = F[gamma] built from the SPATIAL METRIC ALONE fails.
        Proof, using only objects computed above:
          1. the lam = 1 (PG) slice of vacuum Schwarzschild has gamma_ij EXACTLY FLAT (control C2),
             i.e. isometric to a slice of Minkowski;
          2. requirement (d) then forces F[gamma_PG] = F[flat] = 0 -- F cannot tell the PG slice of
             a black hole from a slice of empty space, because it sees only gamma;
          3. requirement (b) propagates that zero to EVERY slice of the same spacetime, so qt = 0
             on the static slice too;
          4. requirement (c) demands c^2|D qt| = g_Newton /= 0 there.  Contradiction.
  [FAIL] T4 [theorem] a functional of the spatial metric alone can satisfy (b), (c) and (d) simultaneously   (it cannot: PG's gamma is exactly flat while the spacetime is not, so (d) forces qt = 0 there, (b) propagates the zero to the static slice, and (c) is then violated.  This kills A1's ENTIRE structure -- a MOND constraint on the conformal mode of gamma -- not merely the choice ln det gamma)

    e1  WHERE THE (b)-SURVIVORS LIVE, and what that does to the count.
        On the PG slice: R3 = 0 exactly, while K = 3*sqrt(2)*sqrt(M)/(2*r**(3/2)) and K_ijK^ij = 9*M/(2*r**3).
        Gauss-Codazzi then says the whole of I1 = 48 M^2/r^6 there is built out of K_ij, i.e. out
        of the gravitational MOMENTUM pi^ij.  So on that slice every (b)-survivor is a function of
        pi alone.
  [FAIL] e1 (e) a (b)-surviving qt leaves C_M momentum-free, so A1's rank-2 argument -- the SOLE source of its 2-DOF claim -- still applies   (it does not: on the PG slice R3 = 0 and all of the curvature sits in K_ij, so delta C_M / delta pi^ij /= 0.  A1's count assumed C_M contains no gravitational momentum; that hypothesis is false for every candidate that passes (b).  A constraint on pi alone is a SLICING condition (the CMC/York type), which is the preferred foliation arriving by the other door)

    e2  THE KILLING-NORM CANDIDATE, judged as hard as the others.  It passes (a), (b), (c), (d).
  [FAIL] e2 (e)+(f) ln sqrt(-xi.xi) is available as a replacement for q: it exists on the spacetimes the theory must describe, and it removes a gravitational degree of freedom   (neither.  (i) EXISTENCE: FLRW has no timelike Killing vector -- nabla_(a xi_b) for xi = d/dt is a(t)*Derivative(a(t), t) /= 0 whenever adot /= 0 -- so the candidate is UNDEFINED on cosmology and on any evolving galaxy; the conformal Killing vector that does exist gives ln a, spatially constant, which is L12's cosmology failure verbatim.  (ii) COUNT: xi is fixed by the whole spacetime, not by (gamma, pi) on one slice, so C_M is not a constraint on the gravitational phase space at all -- it removes NO mode and merely over-determines the lapse, which is a khronon written in geometric clothing.  On the spacetimes where xi does exist, ln sqrt(-xi.xi) IS the static lapse, i.e. candidate b06)

    T5  THE CURVATURE PINCER.  A local curvature scalar splits into Ricci and Weyl parts, and the
        two halves fail on OPPOSITE sides of the problem.
        FLRW: Weyl tensor identically zero = True
        interior Schwarzschild (uniform-density star): max|Weyl| = 4.100e-143 against max|Riemann| = 2.260e-02
  [FAIL] T5 [theorem] some LOCAL curvature scalar can serve as the MOND potential everywhere the theory must work   (no, and the two halves fail on opposite sides.  RICCI half (R4, R_munu R^munu, T, rho): proportional to T_munu, hence exactly ZERO in vacuum -- no field outside a galaxy's baryons, where the rotation curve is.  WEYL half (I1, E^2, C^2, and every ratio built from them): exactly ZERO wherever the spacetime is conformally flat -- verified here for FLRW (Weyl = 0 identically) and for the interior Schwarzschild solution, a uniform-density star (max|Weyl| = 4.1e-143 vs max|Riemann| = 2.3e-02), where |grad Phi| = (4 pi G rho/3) r /= 0.  Newtonianly: inside any homogeneous region the traceless part of d_i d_j Phi vanishes while the force does not)

    T3  THE HOMOGENEITY THEOREM.  This one needs no search at all, and it is the sharpest result
        of the lane: it is INDEPENDENT of what q is.
  [FAIL] T3 [theorem] the constraint C_M = D_i[mu(y) D^i qt] - S ~ 0 admits a nonzero matter density on a homogeneous background, for SOME scalar qt   (for NO scalar qt.  On an exactly homogeneous slice every spatial scalar is spatially constant, so D_i qt = 0, y = 0 and the frozen kernel gives mu(0) = 1 - e^0 = 0: the whole operator vanishes identically (verified symbolically, C_M = 0), leaving C_M = -S and therefore rho = 0 against rho_m = 2.688e-27 kg/m^3 today.  a0 does not appear, so the statement is IDENTICAL on both footings (9.3619e-11 and 1.1279e-10).  A1's cosmology failure is a property of its CONSTRAINT FORM, not of ln det gamma, and no replacement for q can repair it)

    f1  COMPATIBILITY (L12's matter failure), inherited by ANY candidate that satisfies (c).
        canonical : H_perp wants g = 1.3942e-11, C_M wants g = 4.0053e-11 m/s^2 -- factor 2.873
        alt       : H_perp wants g = 1.3942e-11, C_M wants g = 4.3537e-11 m/s^2 -- factor 3.123
  [FAIL] f1 (f) a (c)-satisfying qt is compatible with GR's own Hamiltonian constraint rather than over-determining it   (it is not, and the failure is inherited from (c) itself: any qt that reduces to Phi/c^2 in the static weak field ALREADY obeys grad^2 qt = 4 pi G rho/c^2 by H_perp.  Imposing C_M as well leaves div[e^{-y} grad qt] = 0 with e^{-y} > 0 strictly, whose only decaying solution is qt = const, i.e. g = 0.  The two constraints demand accelerations differing by 2.87x (canonical) / 3.12x (alt) for 1e10 Msun at 10 kpc.  Requirement (c) and requirement (f) are in direct conflict for ANY q)

